  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/id "adr-2607232130-asi-readiness-assessment-etzhayyim-kotoba")
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/last_verified "2026-07-23")
  (:adr/body "# ADR-2607232130: ASI-readiness assessment — etzhayyim and the kotoba language, against DeepMind's \"From AGI to ASI\"\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-23\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (agent-executed, standing authorization)\n**Related**: etzhayyim ADR-2605061200 (AGI/artificial-organism architecture),\netzhayyim ADR-2606271400 (self-evolution leash + consent gate), kotoba\nADR-2607201300 (safe application language profile), ADR-2607198300 (native\nAOT execution tier), ADR-2607198200 (guest-grammar string-interpolation gap /\nStripe pilot), ADR-2607072000 (Rust-avoidance policy / kotoba-server gap),\nADR-2607062330 / ADR-2607062400 (kototama Chicory + browser-native execution).\n\n## Context\n\nThe repository owner asked two questions: (1) is `etzhayyim`, as currently\ndesigned/implemented, an ASI (Artificial Superintelligence) system per\nGoogle's AGI-to-ASI research; and (2) does the kotoba language have the\nconditions in place to be \"a language for ASI\". Both questions require a\nnamed reference framework and grounded evidence from the actual repos, not a\nfree-associated answer.\n\n### Reference framework\n\nGoogle DeepMind has published a trilogy: \"Levels of AGI for Operationalizing\nProgress on the Path to AGI\" (Morris et al., ICML 2024, arXiv:2311.02462—\ndiscrete 6-tier performance × generality axes, No AI → Superhuman, plus a\nseparate 0–5 autonomy axis), an AGI-safety follow-up (2025), and **\"From AGI\nto ASI\"** (Genewein, Franklin, Lerchner, Orseau, Albanie, Bales, Wyeth, Chan,\nGabriel, Leibo, Dafoe, Hutter, Graepel, Legg — DeepMind, arXiv:2606.12683,\nposted 2026-06-10). The 2026 paper is the correct source for an \"AGI to ASI\"\nevaluation: it explicitly does **not** reuse the 2024 paper's discrete-level\ntaxonomy (\"such detailed discussions are beyond the scope of this report\"),\nusing instead a **continuum** grounded in the Legg-Hutter intelligence\nmeasure with Universal AI / AIXI as the theoretical limit.\n\nPer that paper: **AGI** ≈ roughly human-level across most cognitive tasks.\n**ASI** ≈ superhuman across virtually all domains, benchmarked rhetorically\nagainst outperforming \"tens of thousands of well-coordinated expert-level\nhumans working over 10 years with 2010-era technology\" — i.e. it exceeds\nlarge human-expert *collectives*, not just individual experts. Four\nnon-exclusive pathways are named: scaling AGI, a paradigm shift, **recursive\nself-improvement**, and ASI emerging from a **large-scale multi-agent\ncollective**. The paper does not specify hard safety prerequisites for\nreaching ASI; it lists bottlenecks (data wall, economic/resource limits,\nabstraction barrier, deliberate slowdown) as friction, not a design mandate.\nGiven the absence of a formal prerequisite checklist, this ADR evaluates both\nsystems against the two concrete pathway axes the paper does name —\nrecursive self-improvement and autonomous multi-agent collectives — since\nthose are the only structurally falsifiable claims the paper makes about how\nASI would actually arise.\n\n## Decision\n\nRecord, as a standing factual assessment (re-usable the next time this\nquestion comes up, instead of re-deriving it from scratch):\n\n### 1. etzhayyim is not an ASI system, by design\n\n- Zero occurrences of \"superintelligence\" or \"ASI\" (as a real token, not a\n  substring of \"basic\"/\"Asian\") describing the system anywhere in\n  `orgs/etzhayyim/`. \"AGI\" hits are almost entirely false positives (ticker\n  symbols, IRS \"Adjusted Gross Income\", an unrelated BPMN process name).\n- The one substantive hit, etzhayyim ADR-2605061200 (\"AGI / artificial\n  organism architecture\"), is a **forward-looking constraint document**, not\n  a self-description. It lists as a **Prohibited Pattern**: \"Treating a\n  next-token predictor as the full AGI architecture\", and its operative\n  design principle (§7–8) is the opposite of a monolithic superintelligence:\n  an ecosystem of **multiple narrow organisms** under least-privilege\n  boundaries, with runtime/germline/evolution-lab repos kept separate.\n- Self-modification is explicitly bounded, not open-ended: etzhayyim\n  ADR-2606271400 (status: proposed) gates autonomous writes behind a\n  **revocable, member-signed CACAO leash** plus a single human consent gate\n  at PR-merge, and separates low-risk \"publication\" from high-risk\n  \"actuation\" (funds, access grants, deletes, votes) which stays under\n  human/Council gate. ADR-2605061200 §5/§8 additionally forbids a\n  self-evolving system from freely rewriting its own top-level objective\n  function. This directly forecloses the paper's \"recursive self-improvement\"\n  pathway as a designed-in capability.\n- Actual maturity is thin relative to the documentation volume: of the\n  broader etzhayyim ADR corpus, the large majority are `proposed`/`active`\n  rather than `accepted`-and-shipped, and a 2026-06-27 internal fleet probe\n  found only 1 of 24 defined heartbeats actually resident. Some individual\n  actors (e.g. `com-etzhayyim-kazaori`) have real, passing test suites, so\n  this is genuine working software, not vaporware — but nowhere near the\n  scale, autonomy, or capability the \"From AGI to ASI\" paper describes.\n\n### 2. kotoba does not currently meet the conditions for an ASI-hosting language\n\n- Zero occurrences of \"superintelligen\", \"ASI\", \"AGI\", or \"artificial\n  general\" across kotoba's ADRs or `orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba/`.\n  ADR-2607201300 frames kotoba explicitly as a \"safe **application**\n  language\" — product logic, UI, LLM tool-calling, actor workflows — not as\n  ASI-hosting infrastructure. There is no ASI/AGI design goal anywhere to\n  evaluate against; the assessment below is against what such a language\n  would need, not against a claim kotoba makes about itself.\n- **What exists and is real, tested, current**: typed capability values with\n  fail-closed dispatch and a per-call audit journal (a denied host-import\n  call never reaches the provider handler — `kotoba.host-providers`); real\n  memory/fuel quota enforcement via Chicory `withMemoryLimits`\n  (ADR-2607062330); a native AOT backend emitting real x86_64/aarch64\n  machine code with verified trap/signal detection, avoiding a JVM/Node/Rust\n  runtime dependency at execution time (ADR-2607198300); a policy against\n  introducing new Rust execution engines, keeping the implementation surface\n  in `.cljc` (ADR-2607072000).\n- **What is missing entirely**: formal verification / theorem-prover\n  integration (zero hits anywhere in kotoba's docs); language-level\n  capability *revocation* at runtime (the only \"revocation\" hit in the\n  workspace is unrelated Shamir-share key rotation in `kotoba-custody`);\n  sandboxed *self-modification* (no mechanism admits code generating code in\n  the guest grammar); human-in-the-loop gating as a general language\n  primitive (it exists only as a one-off design intent for a single pilot,\n  not something the language enforces).\n- The rigorous S0–S5 capability-safety ladder that once existed (Rust,\n  ~170 tests) was deleted wholesale on 2026-07-01 (ADR-2607072000) and is now\n  explicitly labeled a \"historical Rust design record\" — the replacement CLJ\n  system is real but thinner, and conflating the two overstates current\n  rigor.\n- Baseline language maturity is still pre-1.0: the guest grammar has **no\n  runtime string concatenation/interpolation** (strings are immutable\n  ≤127-byte compile-time literals only, confirmed current as of 2026-07-20 —\n  ADR-2607198200), the native AOT backend does not yet support the\n  `kgraph-assert!`/`kgraph-query` datom-store ops, and ADR-2607198200's own\n  pilot found that most nominal `.kotoba`-using repos in a sampled cohort\n  were actually a different, non-kotoba-parsing schema DSL — real\n  kotoba-language usage is smaller than file-extension counts suggest.\n\n## Verification\n\nGrep sweeps for `superintelligen|\\bASI\\b|\\bAGI\\b|artificial general` across\n`orgs/etzhayyim/` and `orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba/` + `90-docs/adr/*kotoba*`\nreturned zero self-descriptive hits in either tree (etzhayyim's one AGI hit\nis a prohibition, not a claim). Direct reads of etzhayyim ADR-2605061200 §5,\n§7–8 and ADR-2606271400 confirm the leash/consent-gate/no-objective-rewrite\nconstraints as currently documented (status: proposed for the leash ADR, not\nyet `accepted`). Direct reads of kotoba ADR-2607201300, -2607198300,\n-2607198200, -2607072000, -2607062330, -2607062400 confirm the\ncapability/audit/quota mechanisms that are live and tested, and confirm the\ndeletion-and-not-fully-replaced status of the Rust safety ladder.\n\n## Consequences\n\n(+) Establishes a citable, evidence-grounded answer to \"is X ASI / ASI-ready\"\nfor this workspace, referencing the correct current paper (2026\n\"From AGI to ASI\", not the superseded 2024 discrete-level taxonomy), so this\ndoes not need to be re-researched from scratch next time it comes up.\n\n(+) Makes explicit that etzhayyim's narrow-organism-collective +\nleash/consent-gate architecture and kotoba's deny-by-default capability model\nare *already* aligned with avoiding the paper's \"recursive self-improvement\"\nand \"unbounded autonomous collective\" pathways — this is a property worth\npreserving deliberately, not an accidental gap to be closed.\n\n(-) This ADR does **not** authorize starting work to make kotoba\n\"ASI-ready\" (formal verification integration, a language-level capability\nrevocation primitive, sandboxed self-modification). Those would be\nsubstantial, safety-relevant design commitments and must go through their own\ndedicated ADR(s) if the owner chooses to pursue them — not be silently\nbootstrapped off this assessment.\n\n(-) The assessment is a snapshot as of 2026-07-23. Both etzhayyim (fast ADR\nchurn, `proposed`-heavy corpus) and kotoba (active language-maturity work,\ne.g. ADR-2607198200's string-interpolation gap) are moving targets; this ADR\nshould be re-verified rather than assumed evergreen if cited much later.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n| Option | Verdict | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Answer from general knowledge without reading the actual repos/papers | ❌ | Would risk citing the superseded 2024 \"Levels of AGI\" taxonomy instead of the 2026 ASI-specific paper, and would risk describing etzhayyim/kotoba's *aspirational* docs as if they were current implementation |\n| Treat this purely as a chat answer, no ADR | ❌ | Owner explicitly asked for the ADRs involved to be compiled/recorded; a repeatable factual assessment belongs in the ADR corpus like other cross-cutting investigations (e.g. ADR-2607231900's Russian-fleet assessment) |\n| Use this ADR to also kick off ASI-readiness implementation work for kotoba | ❌ | Out of scope — a decision to pursue formal verification / capability revocation / self-mod sandboxing is a separate, larger design commitment that deserves its own ADR and explicit owner sign-off, not scope creep off an assessment ADR |\n\n## References\n\n- DeepMind, \"From AGI to ASI\" (arXiv:2606.12683, 2026-06-10) — https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.12683\n- Morris et al., \"Levels of AGI for Operationalizing Progress on the Path to AGI\" (arXiv:2311.02462, ICML 2024) — https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.02462\n- `orgs/etzhayyim/root/90-docs/adr/2605061200-agi-active-inference-artificial-organism-architecture.edn`\n- `orgs/etzhayyim/root/90-docs/adr/2606271400-self-evolution-leash-and-consent-gate-consolidation.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607201300-kotoba-safe-application-language-profile.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607198300-kotoba-native-aot-execution-tier-discovery-and-no-bypass-policy.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607198200-kotoba-guest-grammar-no-runtime-string-interpolation-stripe-pilot.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607072000-kotoba-rust-avoidance-policy-and-kotoba-server-gap.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607062330-kototama-tender-chicory-execution-runtime.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607062400-kototama-actor-host-browser-native-and-tender-module-fix.edn`\n")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki"))
  (:adr/date "2026-07-23")
  (:db/id -1)
